Done. Summary of what I did:

**Task: Make the emerging (anticipatory) predictions readable back from risk snapshots.**

The snapshot writer already records both top_10 (reactive risk) and emerging (momentum-based "about to become fragile" predictions), but parse_all_snapshots only read back top_10. The anticipatory half — the DREAM's allostatic signal — was write-only and invisible to any reader.

Changes to src/commands_risk_snapshots.rs (one file, additive):

1. Added `emerging: Vec<String>` to `ParsedSnapshot`. No non-test caller consumes it yet (the grading loop is an explicitly-deferred follow-up task), so I annotated it #[allow(dead_code)] with a comment explaining it's threaded through now as a prerequisite for a later grading session — rather than leave it read-only in tests and fail the `-D warnings` clippy gate on the binary target.

2. Extended `parse_all_snapshots` to pull emerging paths the same defensive way as predicted (filter_map on each element's path), keeping the existing if !predicted.is_empty() push guard untouched so backward compat holds: a legacy line with no emerging key yields Value::Null → None → empty vec.

3. Three test-first unit tests: extracts emerging in order, legacy line without emerging parses to empty (backward-compat guarantee), and malformed emerging entries missing path are skipped without panic.

I deliberately did not touch auto_validate_after_failure / write_validation_event — grading the emerging predictions is out of scope per the task's scope guard.

Also updated one clause in CLAUDE.md noting ParsedSnapshot now carries both predicted and emerging.

Verification: cargo fmt, `cargo clippy --all-targets -- -D warnings, cargo build, and full cargo test (88 passed) all green. Committed as 80d7ba5c`.

This is the smallest concrete step toward the DREAM's next milestone — you can't measure a prediction you can't read back. A future session can now compare anticipatory-hit-rate against reactive-hit-rate.
